  MRNA [neutral]: Moderna shares fell 12% in the week to Nov. 13 after the company guided to about $4 billion or more in annual cash costs through 2030, mostly for cancer drugs. The stock is still up over 600% this year following a melanoma headline, and CEO Stéphane Bancel said he wants an FDA filing before the end of the year. ETFs bought $109.7 million of the stock on Nov. 12.
  SNDK [negative]: Sandisk shares fell for a third straight day on November 16, which one headline attributes to the fallout from Nvidia's situation after the departure of Jensen Huang, as the broader market also dropped (the S&P 500 fell 1.9%). Sandisk is scheduled to split its stock 8-for-1 on November 19, and Simply Wall St. reported that analysts trimmed its fair value estimate to US$2,047.38 from US$2,105.29, citing AI demand and industry cyclicality.
  MU [negative]: Micron shares fell sharply on Monday, with one report putting the drop near 7%, after a federal judge let a DRAM supply lawsuit against Micron, Samsung and SK hynix proceed. Headlines also tie the decline to a third straight daily loss, the Nvidia leadership departure, and new U.S. steps to restrict AI-related technology exports to China. One Barron's piece notes that strong expected memory prices next year have not been helping the stock.
  LITE [negative]: Lumentum (LITE) shares fell about 8% on November 16, 2026, as Marvell's stock slide reduced the implied value of Marvell's all-stock takeover of Lumentum to about $657 per share, down from $1,132 when the $115 billion deal was announced on November 2. The broader AI hardware group also fell on reports that Nvidia would rein in AI financing and after leadership turmoil at Nvidia, while the S&P 500 dropped 1.9%.
  DELL [neutral]: Dell shares fell alongside other hardware names after a reported DOJ probe into AI server smuggling to China weighed on Super Micro and dragged Dell and HPE down about 4%. Analysts quoted in the headlines say strong AI and enterprise demand could offset PC weakness, and one article notes Dell's stock has lost more than a third of its value since a recent peak. Dell's financing receivables have also drawn attention, with $4.4 billion in lower-grade customer receivables cited.
  SPCX [neutral]: SpaceX (SPCX) had a major Starship milestone over the weekend, when the upper stage returned to Earth and was caught by the launch tower's mechanical arms. Headlines conflict on the stock reaction: one Barron's piece says SpaceX stock is down after the catch, while a market recap says SpaceX gained after it.
  PLTR [neutral]: Palantir raised its 2026 revenue outlook after third-quarter sales rose 96% on AI demand, according to Zacks. Over the same period, a UK consortium, Drystone Health AI Collaboration, reportedly began mobilizing to challenge Palantir for the £330 million NHS Federated Data Platform contract. Cathie Wood's ARK sold about $11.8 million of Palantir stock, which GuruFocus described as routine rebalancing, and a Motley Fool article noted the stock fell after an analyst upgrade.

